# Konrad Zuse

Konrad Zuse (1910–1995) was a German civil engineer, computer scientist, and inventor who created the world's first programmable digital computer. Working in relative isolation in Berlin during World War II, he designed and constructed the Z3 in 1941, an electromechanical machine that is widely recognized as the first fully functional, Turing-complete automatic computing machine. In addition to his hardware innovations, Zuse formulated Plankalkül in the mid-1940s, which is considered the world's first high-level programming language, although it remained unpublished and unimplemented for several decades. Following the destruction of his early machines during the Allied bombing of Berlin, he salvaged his subsequent machine, the Z4, and established Zuse KG, one of the earliest commercial computer manufacturing companies in the world. Because his work was conducted independently of contemporaneous Allied computer projects—such as the American ENIAC and the British Colossus—Zuse's foundational contributions were largely unrecognized internationally until long after the war. Today, he is celebrated as a fundamental architect of modern computer science, specifically credited with successfully implementing binary arithmetic and floating-point logic in practical computing architecture.

## Early Life and Education

Konrad Zuse was born on June 22, 1910, in Berlin, Germany. His family relocated to East Prussia during his childhood, where he attended the *Humanistisches Gymnasium* in Braunsberg. In 1935, he graduated with a degree in civil engineering from the Technical University of Berlin (then known as the *Technische Hochschule Charlottenburg*).

During his engineering studies, Zuse was frequently burdened by the massive, tedious routine calculations required for determining the static forces of architectural structures. Frustrated by the highly repetitive arithmetic, he theorized that the process could be entirely automated. After graduation, he briefly worked as a stress analyst at the Henschel aircraft factory in Schönefeld. However, he resigned from this position after less than a year to dedicate himself full-time to the construction of an automatic calculating machine, establishing a makeshift workshop in his parents' living room.

## The Z-Series Computers

Operating without state funding or university backing, Zuse relied on independent financing from family and friends to build a series of increasingly sophisticated computing machines throughout the late 1930s and 1940s.

### The Z1 and Z2

In 1938, Zuse completed the Z1, a fully mechanical calculator driven by an electric motor. The Z1 was highly innovative for its time, utilizing a binary numeral system and floating-point arithmetic rather than the decimal systems favored by other early computer pioneers. However, because it was constructed from thousands of hand-cut sheet metal parts, it lacked the mechanical precision necessary for reliable operation and frequently jammed.

To resolve these mechanical reliability issues, Zuse began incorporating electromechanical telephone relays. The Z2, completed in 1940, paired the mechanical memory of the Z1 with an arithmetic and logic unit built entirely from relays. This hybrid machine functioned reliably enough to secure a small amount of funding from the German Aeronautical Research Institute.

### The Z3: The First Working Computer

Zuse's crowning hardware achievement was the Z3, completed in May 1941. It was the world's first working programmable, fully automatic digital computer. Constructed from approximately 2,000 electromechanical relays, it featured a 22-bit word length and read programs from punched 35mm celluloid film. Unlike the later ENIAC, which initially required physical rewiring to change its program, the Z3 was software-programmable. Decades after its creation, the Z3 was mathematically proven to be Turing-complete, cementing its status as the first operational general-purpose computer. Tragically, the original Z3 was destroyed during an Allied bombing raid on Berlin in December 1943.

### The Z4 and Zuse KG

Before the war ended, Zuse began construction on the Z4. As the bombing of Berlin intensified, he evacuated the machine to rural Bavaria, eventually hiding it in a barn in the Alpine village of Hinterstein. In 1949, Zuse founded Zuse KG, one of the first computer startup companies. In 1950, he successfully sold and delivered the Z4 to the Swiss Federal Institute of Technology (ETH Zurich). At the time of its installation, the Z4 was the only operational commercial computer in continental Europe. Zuse KG went on to produce several successful commercial machines, including the Z11 and the Z22 (one of the first computers to use magnetic storage), before the company was eventually acquired by Siemens in the mid-1960s.

## Plankalkül

While isolated in the Bavarian Alps at the end of World War II, Zuse lacked the materials to build hardware and subsequently turned his attention to software. Between 1943 and 1945, he developed Plankalkül (Plan Calculus), a comprehensive, formal system for describing algorithms.

Plankalkül is considered the world's first high-level programming language. It included advanced features that would not appear in mainstream programming languages for years, such as assignment operations, subroutines, conditional statements, arrays, and complex hierarchical data structures. Zuse even utilized Plankalkül to write an algorithm capable of evaluating chess moves. However, because of the chaotic post-war conditions in Germany, he did not publish the full specifications of the language until 1972, and a compiler for it was not successfully implemented until 1998. Consequently, while historically groundbreaking, Plankalkül had minimal direct influence on the development of early languages like FORTRAN or ALGOL.

## Later Life and Legacy

After Zuse KG was absorbed by Siemens in 1964, Zuse retired from the active corporate computer industry. In his later years, he proposed the concept of "calculating space" (*Rechnender Raum*), a theory suggesting that the entire universe operates as a deterministic cellular automaton, an idea that prefigured modern digital physics. He also dedicated much of his time to painting portraits and abstract art, frequently signing his works with the pseudonym "Kuno See."

Zuse received extensive recognition late in his life for his pioneering contributions. He was awarded the Harry H. Goode Memorial Award in 1965 by the IEEE Computer Society and the Werner-von-Siemens-Ring in 1964. In 1999, he was posthumously made a Fellow of the Computer History Museum. He died on December 18, 1995, in Hünfeld, Germany, at the age of 85.

## Works or Publications

* "Der Plankalkül" (1945 manuscript, published fully in 1972) — The formal specification of the first high-level programming language.
* *Rechnender Raum* (Calculating Space) (1969) — A treatise proposing that the universe is the output of a vast computational network.
* *Der Computer – Mein Lebenswerk* (The Computer – My Life) (1984) — Zuse's autobiography detailing the construction of the Z-series machines.

